**user**:
> I have a thick strap of leather (about 4 inches across, a few feet long) that I want to cut the middle out of and reattach so it's shorter. Can I do that with a pair of grommets?
**assistant**:
> You *can* use grommets, but they’re usually not the best choice for structurally shortening a thick leather strap—especially a wide one like 4 inches.
> 
> Here’s the quick breakdown and some better options:
> 
> ## Why grommets alone aren’t ideal
> 
> - Grommets are mainly for **reinforcing holes**, not for taking **tension** across a joint.
> - On a thick strap, especially if it carries load (belt, strap, handle), a grommeted overlap can:
>   - Tear out under tension.
>   - Distort the leather.
>   - Look bulky and a bit improvised.
> - You’d need multiple grommets across the width, and the stress will still concentrate awkwardly.
> 
> If the strap is purely decorative and sees almost no tension, a neat row of grommets could be fine. But if it needs to be strong (e.g., supporting weight, holding something on your body, etc.), I’d use a different method.
> 
> ## Better ways to shorten and reattach
> 
> ### 1. Buckle-style shortening (if adjustability is okay)
> Turn it into an adjustable strap, like a belt:
> 
> 1. **Cut off** the extra length from one end.
> 2. **Punch a slot** or a set of holes in the strap.
> 3. Attach a **buckle** or a similar fastener:
>    - Either:
>      - Fold the leather around the buckle bar and secure it with **rivets** or **stitching**.
>      - Or use a **Chicago screw** (a two-part screw post) through a single hole.
> 4. Punch several holes in the other end for adjustment.
> 
> This is strong, tidy, and familiar.
> 
> ### 2. Overlap with rivets (fixed length, strong)
> 
> If you’re okay with a non-adjustable joint and a small doubled section:
> 
> 1. Cut out the middle segment so that the two ends **overlap** by a few inches (at least 2–3 inches for strength).
> 2. Clamp the overlap so the edges line up nicely.
> 3. Punch **3–4 rivet holes** across the width:
>    - Staggered slightly (a zigzag) if the leather is very thick, to reduce splitting.
> 4. Install **double-cap rivets** or copper rivets.
> 
> This makes a strong, clean mechanical joint. Stronger and less likely to tear than a couple of grommets.
> 
> ### 3. Butt joint with a backer piece
> 
> If you want the strap to remain visually “single layer” from the front:
> 
> 1. Cut the strap to the new length so the ends meet **edge-to-edge** (a butt joint).
> 2. On the **back side**, use a **shorter, narrower backing piece** of leather (say 2–3 inches long, ~2–3 inches wide).
> 3. Place it centered over the joint on the back.
> 4. Rivet or stitch through both layers:
>    - At least 2–3 fasteners on each side of the joint.
>    - From the front, this can look quite neat; from the back, you see the reinforcement plate.
> 
> Stronger and more professional-looking than trying to pull all the load through two grommets.
> 
> ### 4. Stitching (if you’re comfortable with leather sewing)
> 
> Using a **saddle stitch** with waxed thread across an overlap or backer piece will be very strong and flexible. You can add a couple of rivets at the ends for extra security.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## If you really want to use grommets
> 
> You can make a kind of “laced” joint:
> 
> 1. Overlap the two ends.
> 2. Punch **pairs of grommeted holes** along both sides of the overlap.
> 3. Lace them together with strong cord, leather thong, or paracord.
> 
> This can be both decorative and functional if you use enough holes and good lacing, but it’s more complex and bulky than rivets.
> 
> ---
> 
> If you tell me:
> - What the strap is *for* (belt? bag strap? guitar strap? harness?),
> - How thick it is (rough mm or oz if you know),
> 
> I can recommend a specific method and exact hardware sizes (rivet size, hole spacing, etc.), or a step‑by‑step with minimal tools.
