> Do I need any particular tool to cut the leather strap itself?
**assistant**:
> You don’t *need* anything fancy, but a sharp, well‑guided cut matters a lot more than the specific tool.
> 
> ### Easiest decent options
> 
> Use at least one of these:
> 
> 1. **Sharp utility knife / box cutter**
>    - Fresh blade is key.
>    - Use a **metal ruler/straightedge** to guide the cut.
>    - Cut on a **cutting mat or scrap wood**.
>    - Make **multiple light passes** rather than trying to go through in one go—this keeps the cut straighter and safer.
> 
> 2. **Heavy-duty scissors or shears**  
>    - Only if the leather is fairly flexible and not super thick (e.g., under ~6–7 oz / 2.5–3 mm).
>    - Can distort/stretch the edge a bit on thick, stiff leather, so knife is usually better for a clean joint edge.
> 
> ### “Leatherworker” tools (optional, but nice)
> 
> If you want to do it really cleanly:
> 
> - **Head knife / round knife** – traditional, very fast, but needs skill and sharpening.
> - **Strap cutter** – great for cutting long, straight straps from a hide; less necessary if you’re just shortening an existing strap.
> - **Edge beveler** – to round the edges after cutting, if you care about finish.
> 
> ### Basic workflow with a utility knife
> 
> 1. Mark your cut line with pencil or scratch awl.
> 2. Lay a **metal ruler** precisely on that line.
> 3. Hold the ruler down firmly (or clamp it).
> 4. Cut along it with the knife:
>    - First pass: light, just scoring.
>    - Next passes: deepen until you’re through.
> 5. If there are fuzzies or slight unevenness, you can:
>    - Trim with a very light pass.
>    - Sand lightly with fine sandpaper (220–400 grit) if you want it neater.
> 
> If you tell me how thick/stiff the strap is and what tools you already have around the house (box cutters, woodworking tools, etc.), I can suggest the safest/cleanest method with what you’ve got.
